## 3. Database Schema

### Entity Relationship Diagram

```mermaid
erDiagram
    USERS ||--o{ PROFILES : "has many"
    PROFILES ||--o{ TRANSACTIONS : "has many"
    PLATFORMS ||--o{ TRANSACTIONS : "source"
    USERS ||--o{ SHARED_REPORTS : "creates"
    PROFILES ||--o{ GOALS : "targets"

    USERS {
        uuid id PK
        text email
        text display_name
        text preferred_locale
        text preferred_theme
        timestamptz created_at
        timestamptz updated_at
    }

    PROFILES {
        uuid id PK
        uuid user_id FK
        text name
        boolean is_default
        int sort_order
        timestamptz created_at
        timestamptz updated_at
    }

    PLATFORMS {
        uuid id PK
        text name
        text slug
        text icon_url
        jsonb parser_config
        timestamptz created_at
    }

    TRANSACTIONS {
        uuid id PK
        uuid profile_id FK
        uuid platform_id FK
        date transaction_date
        text transaction_type
        numeric amount
        text raw_line
        text import_batch_id
        timestamptz created_at
    }

    GOALS {
        uuid id PK
        uuid profile_id FK
        text title
        text goal_type
        numeric target_value
        date start_date
        date end_date
        boolean is_active
        timestamptz created_at
    }

    SHARED_REPORTS {
        uuid id PK
        uuid user_id FK
        uuid profile_id FK
        text slug
        text title
        jsonb report_config
        boolean is_active
        timestamptz expires_at
        timestamptz created_at
    }
```

### SQL Schema (Drizzle will generate this)

**Key design decisions:**

1. **`users` extends Supabase `auth.users`** — We store app-specific user preferences in a separate `public.users` table linked to `auth.users.id`. Created automatically via trigger on signup.

2. **`transactions.raw_line`** — Stores the original CSV line for auditability and re-parsing.

3. **`transactions.import_batch_id`** — Groups transactions from a single upload for undo/rollback.

4. **`platforms.parser_config`** — JSON config for each platform's CSV format (delimiter, header mappings, date format). Makes new platform support data-driven.

5. **Indexes:**
   - `transactions(profile_id, transaction_date)` — Fast filtered queries
   - `transactions(profile_id, transaction_type)` — Type filtering
   - `transactions(import_batch_id)` — Batch operations
   - `shared_reports(slug)` — Public URL lookup
   - Unique constraint: `transactions(profile_id, transaction_date, transaction_type, amount)` — Duplicate detection

### Row Level Security (RLS)

```sql
-- Users can only see their own data
ALTER TABLE profiles ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "users_own_profiles" ON profiles
  USING (user_id = auth.uid());

ALTER TABLE transactions ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "users_own_transactions" ON transactions
  USING (profile_id IN (
    SELECT id FROM profiles WHERE user_id = auth.uid()
  ));

ALTER TABLE goals ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "users_own_goals" ON goals
  USING (profile_id IN (
    SELECT id FROM profiles WHERE user_id = auth.uid()
  ));

-- Shared reports: owner can CRUD, public can SELECT active ones
ALTER TABLE shared_reports ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "owner_manages_reports" ON shared_reports
  USING (user_id = auth.uid());
CREATE POLICY "public_reads_active_reports" ON shared_reports
  FOR SELECT USING (is_active = true AND (expires_at IS NULL OR expires_at > now()));
```

